s as the hub connecting both comfort measures and safety protocols. [Data: Entities (1524, 3274, 276); Relationships (6429, 3166)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Powerful magnets are fundamental to MRI imaging and drive major safety screening requirements\",\"explanation\":\"POWERFUL MAGNETS are identified as core components of MRI systems, generating the strong magnetic field required for imaging. The data emphasizes that these magnets exert very strong magnetic forces, which creates safety requirements: careful screening and patient disclosure of any metal in or on the body (including implants or embedded fragments), and restricting metal objects from the MRI room to prevent injury or equipment hazards. [Data: Entities (276)]\\n\\nThis is reinforced by the stated relationship that POWERFUL MAGNETS are integral components housed within the MRI MACHINE and are arranged around or partially around the patient depending on design. The relationship explicitly connects the magnetic environment to safety concerns regarding metallic objects or implants posing risks during scanning. [Data: Entities (276, 1524); Relationships (3166)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Earplugs address MRI acoustic noise and represent a straightforward patient comfort/hearing-protection measure\",\"explanation\":\"EARPLUGS are described as hearing-protection items offered during MRI scans because MRI machines produce loud noises; their purpose is to protect hearing and improve comfort throughout the scanning process. This frames earplugs as a standard mitigation for a predictable scanning stressor (noise). [Data: Entities (3274); Relationships (6429)]\\n\\nThe relationship data ties earplugs directly to the patient experience during scanning, stating that the patient may wear earplugs due to loud noise. In this community, earplugs are the primary explicit “patient-facing” accessory linked to the MRI environment. [Data: Relationships (6429); Entities (3274)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Open MRI is a distinct MRI machine configuratio